  "response": "**Setup (Bayes' theorem):**\n\n- Prevalence: P(D) = 1/500 = 0.002, so P(¬D) = 0.998\n- Sensitivity: P(+|D) = 0.98\n- Specificity: P(−|¬D) = 0.95, so false positive rate P(+|¬D) = 0.05\n\n**Calculation:**\n\nP(+) = P(+|D)·P(D) + P(+|¬D)·P(¬D)\nP(+) = (0.98 × 0.002) + (0.05 × 0.998)\nP(+) = 0.00196 + 0.0499\nP(+) = 0.05186\n\nP(D|+) = P(+|D)·P(D) / P(+) = 0.00196 / 0.05186 ≈ 0.037794\n\n**Result:** Even with a positive test, there's only about a **3.78%** chance the person actually has the condition — this is a classic illustration of how a low base rate can dominate the outcome even for a fairly accurate test (most positives are false positives from the large healthy population).\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
